{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017bVGt8teysC2kKPHbmkxm3","type":"tool_result","content":"7:**G6.** A ``record_id`` with a ``commit`` envelope in a per-PID journal (or the\n8:aggregate journal) and ``wall_ns >= since`` that is neither in the canonical log\n10:in transit is never called lost: (0) the journals; (1) a FRESH listing of the\n17:``_RID_RX``. Lower bound: a ``commit`` that never left the in-memory journal\n18:buffer, one whose journal compaction removed, or one TRUNCATED on disk (it is a\n19:line that does not parse: only ``journal_malformed_lines`` sees it) is invisible.\n30:A journal that vanishes is counted apart (``journals_vanished``): it can only\n31:shrink the commit set, never create a loss.\n45:though the exit code ignores it: ``g6_journal_malformed_lines`` — at least one\n46:journal line that does not parse, e.g. a truncated ``commit``, whose record G6\n76:AGGREGATE_JOURNAL = \"audit-pending.journal\"\n125:def journal_commits(st: ss.ReadOnlyDir, since_ns: int) -> Tuple[Set[str], int, int]:\n126:    commits: Set[str] = set()\n130:        if name != AGGREGATE_JOURNAL and not ss.NAME_PATTERNS[\"journal\"].fullmatch(name):\n140:            if (isinstance(env, dict) and env.get(\"op\") == \"commit\"\n143:                commits.add(env[\"record_id\"])\n144:    return commits, malformed, len(vanished)\n180:    commits, malformed, journals_vanished = journal_commits(st, since_ns)\n181:    lost = set(commits)\n202:    return {\"commits\": len(commits), \"pending\": len(pending), \"quarantined\": len(quarantined),\n207:            \"journals_vanished\": journals_vanished, \"journal_malformed_lines\": malformed,\n267:    if rep.get(\"g6\", {}).get(\"journal_malformed_lines\"):\n268:        rep[\"attention\"].append(\"g6_journal_malformed_lines\")"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b7193d9-2764-4133-b585-5164ec8d2baf","uuid":"eb4e7f5b-ada0-4e31-af6a-e1146c70d57f","timestamp":"2026-11-13T17:57:28.056Z","tool_use_result":{"mode":"content","numFiles":0,"filenames":[],"content":"7:**G6.** A ``record_id`` with a ``commit`` envelope in a per-PID journal (or the\n8:aggregate journal) and ``wall_ns >= since`` that is neither in the canonical log\n10:in transit is never called lost: (0) the journals; (1) a FRESH listing of the\n17:``_RID_RX``. Lower bound: a ``commit`` that never left the in-memory journal\n18:buffer, one whose journal compaction removed, or one TRUNCATED on disk (it is a\n19:line that does not parse: only ``journal_malformed_lines`` sees it) is invisible.\n30:A journal that vanishes is counted apart (``journals_vanished``): it can only\n31:shrink the commit set, never create a loss.\n45:though the exit code ignores it: ``g6_journal_malformed_lines`` — at least one\n46:journal line that does not parse, e.g. a truncated ``commit``, whose record G6\n76:AGGREGATE_JOURNAL = \"audit-pending.journal\"\n125:def journal_commits(st: ss.ReadOnlyDir, since_ns: int) -> Tuple[Set[str], int, int]:\n126:    commits: Set[str] = set()\n130:        if name != AGGREGATE_JOURNAL and not ss.NAME_PATTERNS[\"journal\"].fullmatch(name):\n140:            if (isinstance(env, dict) and env.get(\"op\") == \"commit\"\n143:                commits.add(env[\"record_id\"])\n144:    return commits, malformed, len(vanished)\n180:    commits, malformed, journals_vanished = journal_commits(st, since_ns)\n181:    lost = set(commits)\n202:    return {\"commits\": len(commits), \"pending\": len(pending), \"quarantined\": len(quarantined),\n207:            \"journals_vanished\": journals_vanished, \"journal_malformed_lines\": malformed,\n267:    if rep.get(\"g6\", {}).get(\"journal_malformed_lines\"):\n268:        rep[\"attention\"].append(\"g6_journal_malformed_lines\")","numLines":23,"totalLines":23},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017bVGt8teysC2kKPHbmkxm3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1MnXJHjbL5NpxJ2uMBb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018cNrubZPagKfEnNEeqaW7h","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","offset":676,"limit":125},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1123,"cache_read_input_tokens":257768,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1123},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b7193d9-2764-4133-b585-5164ec8d2baf","uuid":"7c1c7057-c02b-45b1-8b84-17be80423d3e","timestamp":"2026-11-13T17:57:30.007Z","request_id":"req_011Ch1MnX5QAMhtmH62DgTqw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_018cNrubZPagKfEnNEeqaW7h":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","offset":676,"limit":125}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018cNrubZPagKfEnNEeqaW7h","type":"tool_result","content":"676\t  `CC_LEDGER_UNREADABLE` (unknown), a component whose check raises is a\n677\t  named `COMPONENT_CHECK_ERROR` (unknown), and in both cases `--strict`\n678\t  still exits 1 on the other components' drift. This cures a P2 finding of\n679\t  the v1.4.2 GA re-pass. `/ceo-boot` does not run this tool.\n680\t\n681\t### Added — read-only audit verifiers\n682\t\n683\t- `.claude/scripts/audit_spool_state.py` inspects the audit spool's state\n684\t  directory.\n685\t- `.claude/scripts/check-audit-real-loss.py` reports a lower bound of real\n686\t  event loss.\n687\t- Both are read-only. Their limits are in Known-open below.\n688\t\n689\t### Changed — other files that reach installs\n690\t\n691\t- `_lib/spool_writer.py`: on the first event of a new spool, the header no\n692\t  longer gets its own `fsync`. The event's `fsync` on the same file covers\n693\t  the header, before the commit and the success signal, so one `fsync`\n694\t  replaces two. If that `fsync` fails, the event can later appear twice\n695\t  (low severity). This change came through a pull-request merge (Known-open\n696\t  below).\n697\t- `ceo-cost-transcripts.py` gains a model-routing detector. It compares\n698\t  the model that served each call with the declared one, VETO floor\n699\t  included. It also gains an `--assert-routing` gate, with exit codes 0, 1\n700\t  and 3.\n701\t- `generate-skill-inventory.sh` now passes its texts to `python3` through\n702\t  the environment instead of argv. Under an EDR agent on macOS, the argv\n703\t  route ended in rc 137.\n704\t- Two scripts changed for the framework's CI. Both reach installs, because\n705\t  the installer copies every top-level `.claude/scripts/*.py`:\n706\t  - `goap-planner.py`, the planner behind `/goap`, now caches where it\n707\t    found the hook directory.\n708\t  - The new `diagnose-output-hook.py` is a CI diagnostic that nothing in an\n709\t    install runs.\n710\t- The substrate ledger (`.claude/scripts/substrate-watch.json`) records\n711\t  Claude Code 2.1.295 and Codex CLI 0.160.0. Only `upgrade.sh` delivers\n712\t  this file. The install floor stays at Claude Code 2.1.280.\n713\t- Framework repository docs (not delivered to installs): `SUPPORT.md` and\n714\t  `docs/adopter-new-model-fast-access.md` now cite the Claude Code 2.1.284\n715\t  changelog: `ultracode` no longer forces `xhigh` effort. The effort\n716\t  defaults they cite were read in the 2.1.295 binary, not probed on it.\n717\t\n718\t### Changed — pair-rail reviewer re-pinned to Codex CLI 0.160.0 (PLAN-194, ADR-182)\n719\t\n720\t- The payload manifest pins the 0.160.0 binary by sha256, for one platform\n721\t  only (`aarch64-apple-darwin`). The accepted range becomes\n722\t  `>=0.128.0,<0.161.0`: the upper bound is widened, and the lower bound is\n723\t  unchanged.\n724\t- In the framework checkout, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` fails\n725\t  CLOSED on any other binary.\n726\t- Rail measurements taken before and after the re-pin are not comparable,\n727\t  because the instrument changed.\n728\t- Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`. There the pin resolves as\n729\t  infrastructure and fails open (advisory), whatever the Codex version.\n730\t  That is unchanged.\n731\t- With `--harness codex`, the installer's version-skew warning reads the\n732\t  same range. So Codex 0.160.x now counts as in range for Codex as a hook\n733\t  host, while the hook fixtures stay at 0.139.0 (Known-open below).\n734\t\n735\t### Changed — the framework's own CI and release (no installed file changes)\n736\t\n737\t- The npm package is now published with Node.js 24 and exactly npm\n738\t  11.20.0. Before, it used `^11.5.1` on Node 20.\n739\t- An rc tag runs a toolchain proof that never publishes: no `id-token`, and\n740\t  `npm pack --dry-run`.\n741\t- For whoever installs, nothing changes: the shim still needs Node.js 20 or\n742\t  later.\n743\t- On `main` pushes, scheduled runs and manual dispatches, while the\n744\t  repository variable `VM_CI_ENABLED` is `1` (set on 2026-10-11), the jobs\n745\t  of `validate.yml`, `coverage.yml`, `ownership-nightly.yml` and\n746\t  `smoke-install.yml` run on the maintainer's self-hosted runner group,\n747\t  except the 3.14 leg. Pull requests, and those runs when the variable is\n748\t  off, use GitHub-hosted runners. These changes came through pull-request\n749\t  merges (Known-open below).\n750\t- Off that route, the Python 3.9 test leg runs on the fixed `ubuntu-24.04`\n751\t  label. `setup-python` has no 3.9 for Ubuntu 26.04, and `ubuntu-latest`\n752\t  moves to 26.04 during a rollout that starts on 2026-10-19.\n753\t- A 3.14 leg runs on `ubuntu-26.04`, on schedule only.\n754\t- The CI template the installer delivers (`validate.yml.template`, on\n755\t  `ubuntu-latest`) does not change. Its steps were measured 10/10 green in\n756\t  an `ubuntu:26.04` container.\n757\t- Formal verification: `docs/formal-verification/properties-proved.md` now\n758\t  opens with a correction. The April property inventory is not a statement\n759\t  that the old model passed TLC, and the model's first real TLC run found a\n760\t  violation (`S2_HalfOpenSingleton`). The breaker model was rewritten; the\n761\t  runtime did not change. `formal-verify.yml` now runs TLC on the three\n762\t  models, plus regression and mutation checks of the breaker model. The\n763\t  job stays advisory, so a red check does not turn the run red. These are\n764\t  results on a finite model, not a proof.\n765\t\n766\t### Known-open (declared by this release)\n767\t\n768\t- **The audit spool.**\n769\t  - It leaves per-process lock and journal files in its state directory,\n770\t    and nothing removes them.\n771\t  - The forced drain at a hook's exit can wait on the canonical lock longer\n772\t    than the hook's timeout. Claude Code discards the decision of a hook\n773\t    that exceeds its timeout, even when the hook's output was already\n774\t    written.\n775\t  - This release carries neither that cure nor the stress run that would\n776\t    prove it.\n777\t- **The new verifiers have limits.**\n778\t  - The real-loss verifier reports a lower bound: a record whose commit\n779\t    never reached the on-disk journal, or was compacted away or truncated\n780\t    there, is not seen.\n781\t  - It can report a silent zero when the errors file disappears and an\n782\t    empty one takes its name.\n783\t  - Counters for forms that only the spool cure introduces read 0 by\n784\t    design, and both verifiers cite a design amendment this release does\n785\t    not ship.\n786\t  - No shipped tool removes the stock of empty per-process files.\n787\t- **Audit-file modes.** Some audit-family files are created by an `open()`\n788\t  that sets no mode, so they take the umask's mode (0644 was observed), not\n789\t  0600. Among them: an audit log that the spool drain's rotation creates,\n790\t  the errors file, a compacted spool journal, and a fallback breadcrumb\n791\t  file under `/tmp`.\n792\t- **The condition-67 cure has declared limits.**\n793\t  - Breaks the race already wrote stay in the live log and in the rotated\n794\t    archives: this release rewrites no history.\n795\t  - Under lock saturation, the longer critical section can time out and\n796\t    drop the line (fail-open, breadcrumb only).\n797\t  - After a failed marker write, the next line another writer signs chains\n798\t    to the missing marker, and `verify_chain()` reports a break there.\n799\t  - `append_entry` does not increment the `audit-log.chain-length` counter,\n800\t    so tail-truncation detection does not count `agent_spawn` lines."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b7193d9-2764-4133-b585-5164ec8d2baf","uuid":"da3cac94-6376-4383-a339-9e6c18265c7e","timestamp":"2026-11-13T17:57:30.030Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/CHANGELOG.md","content":"  `CC_LEDGER_UNREADABLE` (unknown), a component whose check raises is a\n  named `COMPONENT_CHECK_ERROR` (unknown), and in both cases `--strict`\n  still exits 1 on the other components' drift. This cures a P2 finding of\n  the v1.4.2 GA re-pass. `/ceo-boot` does not run this tool.\n\n### Added — read-only audit verifiers\n\n- `.claude/scripts/audit_spool_state.py` inspects the audit spool's state\n  directory.\n- `.claude/scripts/check-audit-real-loss.py` reports a lower bound of real\n  event loss.\n- Both are read-only. Their limits are in Known-open below.\n\n### Changed — other files that reach installs\n\n- `_lib/spool_writer.py`: on the first event of a new spool, the header no\n  longer gets its own `fsync`. The event's `fsync` on the same file covers\n  the header, before the commit and the success signal, so one `fsync`\n  replaces two. If that `fsync` fails, the event can later appear twice\n  (low severity). This change came through a pull-request merge (Known-open\n  below).\n- `ceo-cost-transcripts.py` gains a model-routing detector. It compares\n  the model that served each call with the declared one, VETO floor\n  included. It also gains an `--assert-routing` gate, with exit codes 0, 1\n  and 3.\n- `generate-skill-inventory.sh` now passes its texts to `python3` through\n  the environment instead of argv. Under an EDR agent on macOS, the argv\n  route ended in rc 137.\n- Two scripts changed for the framework's CI. Both reach installs, because\n  the installer copies every top-level `.claude/scripts/*.py`:\n  - `goap-planner.py`, the planner behind `/goap`, now caches where it\n    found the hook directory.\n  - The new `diagnose-output-hook.py` is a CI diagnostic that nothing in an\n    install runs.\n- The substrate ledger (`.claude/scripts/substrate-watch.json`) records\n  Claude Code 2.1.295 and Codex CLI 0.160.0. Only `upgrade.sh` delivers\n  this file. The install floor stays at Claude Code 2.1.280.\n- Framework repository docs (not delivered to installs): `SUPPORT.md` and\n  `docs/adopter-new-model-fast-access.md` now cite the Claude Code 2.1.284\n  changelog: `ultracode` no longer forces `xhigh` effort. The effort\n  defaults they cite were read in the 2.1.295 binary, not probed on it.\n\n### Changed — pair-rail reviewer re-pinned to Codex CLI 0.160.0 (PLAN-194, ADR-182)\n\n- The payload manifest pins the 0.160.0 binary by sha256, for one platform\n  only (`aarch64-apple-darwin`). The accepted range becomes\n  `>=0.128.0,<0.161.0`: the upper bound is widened, and the lower bound is\n  unchanged.\n- In the framework checkout, `check_pair_rail.py --verify-codex-pin` fails\n  CLOSED on any other binary.\n- Rail measurements taken before and after the re-pin are not comparable,\n  because the instrument changed.\n- Installs do not receive `.claude/governance/`. There the pin resolves as\n  infrastructure and fails open (advisory), whatever the Codex version.\n  That is unchanged.\n- With `--harness codex`, the installer's version-skew warning reads the\n  same range. So Codex 0.160.x now counts as in range for Codex as a hook\n  host, while the hook fixtures stay at 0.139.0 (Known-open below).\n\n### Changed — the framework's own CI and release (no installed file changes)\n\n- The npm package is now published with Node.js 24 and exactly npm\n  11.20.0. Before, it used `^11.5.1` on Node 20.\n- An rc tag runs a toolchain proof that never publishes: no `id-token`, and\n  `npm pack --dry-run`.\n- For whoever installs, nothing changes: the shim still needs Node.js 20 or\n  later.\n- On `main` pushes, scheduled runs and manual dispatches, while the\n  repository variable `VM_CI_ENABLED` is `1` (set on 2026-10-11), the jobs\n  of `validate.yml`, `coverage.yml`, `ownership-nightly.yml` and\n  `smoke-install.yml` run on the maintainer's self-hosted runner group,\n  except the 3.14 leg. Pull requests, and those runs when the variable is\n  off, use GitHub-hosted runners. These changes came through pull-request\n  merges (Known-open below).\n- Off that route, the Python 3.9 test leg runs on the fixed `ubuntu-24.04`\n  label. `setup-python` has no 3.9 for Ubuntu 26.04, and `ubuntu-latest`\n  moves to 26.04 during a rollout that starts on 2026-10-19.\n- A 3.14 leg runs on `ubuntu-26.04`, on schedule only.\n- The CI template the installer delivers (`validate.yml.template`, on\n  `ubuntu-latest`) does not change. Its steps were measured 10/10 green in\n  an `ubuntu:26.04` container.\n- Formal verification: `docs/formal-verification/properties-proved.md` now\n  opens with a correction. The April property inventory is not a statement\n  that the old model passed TLC, and the model's first real TLC run found a\n  violation (`S2_HalfOpenSingleton`). The breaker model was rewritten; the\n  runtime did not change. `formal-verify.yml` now runs TLC on the three\n  models, plus regression and mutation checks of the breaker model. The\n  job stays advisory, so a red check does not turn the run red. These are\n  results on a finite model, not a proof.\n\n### Known-open (declared by this release)\n\n- **The audit spool.**\n  - It leaves per-process lock and journal files in its state directory,\n    and nothing removes them.\n  - The forced drain at a hook's exit can wait on the canonical lock longer\n    than the hook's timeout. Claude Code discards the decision of a hook\n    that exceeds its timeout, even when the hook's output was already\n    written.\n  - This release carries neither that cure nor the stress run that would\n    prove it.\n- **The new verifiers have limits.**\n  - The real-loss verifier reports a lower bound: a record whose commit\n    never reached the on-disk journal, or was compacted away or truncated\n    there, is not seen.\n  - It can report a silent zero when the errors file disappears and an\n    empty one takes its name.\n  - Counters for forms that only the spool cure introduces read 0 by\n    design, and both verifiers cite a design amendment this release does\n    not ship.\n  - No shipped tool removes the stock of empty per-process files.\n- **Audit-file modes.** Some audit-family files are created by an `open()`\n  that sets no mode, so they take the umask's mode (0644 was observed), not\n  0600. Among them: an audit log that the spool drain's rotation creates,\n  the errors file, a compacted spool journal, and a fallback breadcrumb\n  file under `/tmp`.\n- **The condition-67 cure has declared limits.**\n  - Breaks the race already wrote stay in the live log and in the rotated\n    archives: this release rewrites no history.\n  - Under lock saturation, the longer critical section can time out and\n    drop the line (fail-open, breadcrumb only).\n  - After a failed marker write, the next line another writer signs chains\n    to the missing marker, and `verify_chain()` reports a break there.\n  - `append_entry` does not increment the `audit-log.chain-length` counter,\n    so tail-truncation detection does not count `agent_spawn` lines.","numLines":125,"startLine":676,"totalLines":2658}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018cNrubZPagKfEnNEeqaW7h","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
